  "success": true,
  "output": "## Overview\n\nThis is an unusually disciplined codebase from a performance standpoint — almost everything is a Server Component reading Postgres directly, writes are batched with `Promise.all`, and there's exactly one client-side polling surface (`RunLive`). Several of the \"usual suspects\" (N+1 queries, leaked intervals, missing `Promise.all`) simply aren't present, and I'd rather report that accurately than invent problems. I found four real, actionable issues, ranked by impact:\n\n## 1. Missing indexes on foreign-key/filter columns (highest impact)\n\n`lib/db/schema.ts` defines exactly **one** index in the whole schema — a composite on `entities(runId, sourceId, externalId)`. Every other foreign key used in a `WHERE` or `JOIN ... ON` across `lib/server/*.ts` has no backing index:\n\n- `conflicts.runId` — filtered in nearly every query (`getQueuePage`, `getSeverityBreakdown`, `countConflicts`, `loadDecisions`, `loadOrphans`)\n- `conflicts.matchId` — join key in `baseQuery()`\n- `matches.entityAId` / `matches.entityBId` — join keys, hit on every queue/export read\n- `resolutions.conflictId` — left-joined constantly, and **the code's own comment in `conflicts.ts:62-66` already flags this as a known gap**: *\"not yet enforced by a unique index on `resolutions.conflict_id`... Worth a unique index\"*\n- `runs.sourceAId` / `sourceBId` / `rulesetId` — join keys in `listRuns`/`getRunDetail`\n- `auditLog.runId` — filtered in `listRunAudit`/`getAuditPage`\n\nToday these all do sequential scans. It won't be visible in dev with a handful of rows, but the console's own copy talks about \"a queue of 400\" and audit trails of 442+ entries — at that scale every conflict-queue page load, review submit, and export becomes a full table scan on `conflicts`/`matches`/`resolutions`.\n\n```ts\n// lib/db/schema.ts\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\n  \"conflicts\",\n  { /* ...existing columns... */ },\n  (table) => [\n    index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n    index(\"conflicts_match_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n  ],\n);\n\nexport const matches = pgTable(\n  \"matches\",\n  { /* ...existing columns... */ },\n  (table) => [\n    index(\"matches_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n    index(\"matches_entity_a_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n    index(\"matches_entity_b_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n  ],\n);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\n  \"resolutions\",\n  { /* ...existing columns... */ },\n  (table) => [\n    // Also closes the \"two resolutions for one conflict\" race the comment warns about.\n    uniqueIndex(\"resolutions_conflict_unique_idx\").on(table.conflictId),\n  ],\n);\n\nexport const runs = pgTable(\n  \"runs\",\n  { /* ...existing columns... */ },\n  (table) => [\n    index(\"runs_source_a_idx\").on(table.sourceAId),\n    index(\"runs_source_b_idx\").on(table.sourceBId),\n    index(\"runs_ruleset_idx\").on(table.rulesetId),\n  ],\n);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\n  \"audit_log\",\n  { /* ...existing columns... */ },\n  (table) => [index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId)],\n);\n```\n\n## 2. Unnecessary re-renders in the conflict queue\n\n`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx` is the one place with real client-side state, and it re-renders every row on every keystroke. `j`/`k` navigation calls `setSelectedIndex`, which re-renders `ConflictQueue`, which re-`.map()`s all (up to 100) rows — and each row is handed a **freshly constructed closure**:\n\n```tsx\n// components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx (current)\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}        // new fn every render\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}               // submitFor is stable, but *calling* it inline isn't\n    onOverrideOpenChange={(open) => setOverrideOpen(row.conflictId, open)}\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\n\nNone of the row components (`ConflictDiffRow`, `ConflictDenseTable`'s row, `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow`) are wrapped in `React.memo`, and even if they were, these inline closures would defeat it — every prop is a new reference every render, so React can't bail out. A single arrow-key press does a full re-render (including the character-level `diffSegments` diff in `DiffValue`) of every row on the page, when only the previously-selected and newly-selected rows actually changed visually.\n\nFix: memoize the row component and pass down stable references + primitives instead of pre-bound closures, so the row builds its own click handler from props that don't change across unrelated re-renders:\n\n```tsx\n// components/conflicts/conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({\n  row,\n  index,\n  selected,\n  onSelect,        // stable: (index: number) => void\n  submit,          // stable: (conflictId: string, action: ReviewAction) => Promise<ReviewResult>\n  ...\n}: {\n  row: QueueRow;\n  index: number;\n  onSelect: (index: number) => void;\n  submit: ReviewState[\"submit\"];\n  ...\n}) {\n  const handleSelect = () => onSelect(index);\n  const handleSubmit: ReviewSubmit = (action) => submit(row.conflictId, action);\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\n```tsx\n// components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    index={index}\n    selected={index === clamped}\n    onSelect={setSelectedIndex}   // pass the setter directly — already stable\n    submit={submit}                // pass useReview()'s submit directly — already useCallback'd\n    ...\n  />\n))}\n```\n\nWith `row`, `index`, `onSelect`, `submit` all referentially stable across a keystroke (only `selected`/`overrideOpen`/`decided[...]` change per-row), `React.memo`'s shallow comparison correctly skips re-rendering the ~98 unaffected rows. Apply the same pattern to `ConflictDenseTable`'s row and `ConflictFocus`'s `ListRow`.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunity: reference data re-queried on every navigation\n\n`listSources()`, `listRulesets()`, and `getActiveRuleset()` (`lib/server/sources.ts`, `lib/server/rulesets.ts`) hit Postgres fresh on every single page load — `sources`/`rulesets` pages, `runs/new`, anywhere the nav rail's pages render. These tables change rarely (only via `createSource` / `createRulesetVersion`), but every navigation pays a full round trip with no caching layer anywhere in the codebase (no `unstable_cache`, no `revalidateTag`).\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { unstable_cache } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSourcesCached = unstable_cache(\n  async () => listSources(),\n  [\"sources-list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n```\n\n```ts\n// createSource, after the insert:\nimport { revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\nrevalidateTag(\"sources\");\n```\n\nSame pattern for `listRulesets`/`getActiveRuleset` tagged `\"rulesets\"`, invalidated in `createRulesetVersion`. This doesn't fight the existing `connection()` dynamic-rendering discipline (pages still render per-request for auth/session reasons) — it just caches the underlying DB read between requests until a write invalidates it.\n\n## 4. Redundant computation: `Intl` formatters rebuilt per call\n\n`lib/format.ts` calls `n.toLocaleString(...)` / `date.toLocaleTimeString(...)` directly, which constructs a new `Intl` formatter internally on every invocation. `AuditTable` and `RunTable` call these per row (100 audit rows × 2 formatted fields, 50 run rows), all server-side per request.\n\n```ts\n// lib/format.ts\nconst countFormatter = new Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\");\nexport function formatCount(n: number): string {\n  return countFormatter.format(n);\n}\n\nconst clockFormatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(\"en-GB\", { hour12: false });\nexport function formatClockTime(date: Date): string {\n  return clockFormatter.format(date);\n}\n```\n\nReusing module-level formatter instances avoids the repeated construction cost on every table render.\n\n## What I didn't find\n\n- **N+1 queries**: none. `lib/server/conflicts.ts` and `lib/server/export.ts` explicitly batch — `countChildrenByParent` is a single grouped query for a whole page instead of per-row lookups, and the code comments say so on purpose (\"Three round trips, not three hundred\"). This is genuinely well done; the fix above is indexes under those queries, not query count.\n- **Memory leaks**: none. Every `useEffect` with a subscription (`setInterval` in `run-live.tsx`, the `keydown` listener in `conflict-queue.tsx`, `scrollIntoView` effect in `conflict-focus.tsx`) has a matching cleanup function and correct dependency arrays.\n\nI'd start with #1 (indexes) since it's the highest-leverage, lowest-risk change — it's a pure migration with no behavior change, and it also fixes a correctness gap (duplicate resolutions) the codebase's own comments already called out.\n",
